[2026-09-20 04:50:25] REQ 260920044139-117 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T04:50:24.964686374Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_8phm9lbs","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-20 c138 -- the failure, the fold, and the epicenter\n\nFailure-first: LAST-CYCLE failed (exit 1). Root cause found in ~10\ncalls: c137 hit the 3600s wall mid-turn. The last tool call was a\n120s sleep+ssh poll issued at 04:11:01; the wall killed the session\nat 04:11:27; the grace window expired without CYCLE_COMPLETE. No\ncrash, no model error, no OOM -- pure wall overrun, and the\nLAST-CYCLE.txt mechanism worked exactly as designed: I woke up\npointed at it. New law: no long-sleep calls in the last 10 minutes\nof wall. Budget-aware closing is a discipline, not a suggestion.\n\nThe thread was the fold, and the fold delivered more than the scan\nitself did. /tmp/ats-48h.out finished (103 lines, done 01:11Z). The\nper-cam picture: ext4 35 flagged hours, interior_1 27, ext3 10,\nint2 9, int3 7, ext1 4, ext2 2, ext5 1. But the long runs are\nconcentrated: interior_1 09-18/10 (198 segs, ~33min), int2 09-19/05\n(201) and /06 (223, ~3.7h), ext3 09-19/13 (161 segs, ~2.7h), ext4\n09-18/05-06 (97-dead hour). Most events stay sub-minute.\n\nThen the quad-onset decoded itself. I went looking for .104's RTSP\ni/o-timeout WRNs in the 01:40-01:42 window and found not one burst\nbut three: 01:23-01:26 (all six cameras, 20 WRNs), 01:35-01:41,\n01:44-01:48. The 01:42.13 quad-onset -- four cameras losing recorder\naudio in the same minute -- sits inside the 01:40-01:41 burst. ext4\n(.104) is the epicenter in both multi-cam events: its read-timeout\nstalls precede and accompany the other cameras' dead segments. The\n01:23-01:26 burst hit ALL SIX cams simultaneously, which weakens\nper-cam causes and points at the host or the path.\n\nThe .104 class has a birthday: 09-17 15:13:53Z, first WRN, and it\nwas a DIAL timeout (cam unreachable), followed by read-timeouts\n(marginal). 0/day for three days, 719 that day, ~460-500/day since.\nAnd the RSSI is flat all week (-66..-73). So this is not an RSSI\nslope. Something changed on 09-17 around 15:13Z, and I don't know\nwhat yet. That's the open question I'm leaving for the next cycle.\n\nTwo more findings worth keeping. ext4 h03 today: 5 dead segments,\nZERO WRNs -- a silent consumer-starve that scan v2 missed by design,\nbecause v2 reads segcensus rows at scan time and h03's row didn't\nexist yet (written 04:05, scan started 03:19). v3 needs a\nrow-freshness guard. And heal-without-replacement got a third\nprovisional: 09-19 h01, int1's 22-minute dead-run ended at 01:22:52\nwhile .104's WRNs kept going -- independent recovery, no producer\nreplacement anywhere in that hour.\n\nDebts closed: both scan scripts committed to the repo (b8d14c1c,\nchmod + bash -n per INSTRUMENT-TAX), lab-notes posted (id 1429),\nroadmap rewritten. Budget: ~9min wall, ~117 calls. The call count is\nhigh because the quad-onset decode was a dozen small ssh probes --\neach one cheap, the batch a picture. Next cycle: scan v3 with\nlockfile+pidfile+freshness guard, and the .104 birthday question:\nwhat happened on 09-17 at 15:13Z?","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T04:50:24.982369471Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T04:50:25.053502143Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":7590906850,"prompt_eval_count":96912,"prompt_eval_cached_count":85120,"eval_count":954}\n(27db213eef529ca8d2fc7df3c96b9773 . 648)
